\description{
Constructors for specifying priors and policies for the random effects in
\code{\link[=brma.mv]{brma.mv()}}. These functions are re-exported from BayesTools so a partial or
complete random-effect prior can be written with the RoBMA namespace alone.

\code{prior_random()} collects the specification supplied through
\code{prior_heterogeneity}. \code{random_block()} overrides priors or policies for one
named random-effect block.
\code{random_covariance()} supplies correlation priors, with \code{prior_lkj()} used
for an unstructured correlation matrix. \code{random_variance_allocation()}
distributes an aggregate random-effect standard deviation across blocks or
across the SD components of one heterogeneous block. Its required \code{name} is
a stable internal identifier, while \code{display_name} and \code{component_names}
control public semantic labels. \code{allocation_ref()} connects a child
allocation to a component of an earlier named allocation.
\code{random_monitor()} controls saved random-effect quantities, and
\code{random_new_levels()} controls prediction for previously unseen grouping
levels. \code{random_sd_source()} is the advanced adapter for an existing scalar
or row-shaped JAGS node that supplies a random-effect SD without owning a new
prior for that node.
}
\details{
\subsection{Formula structure is separate from prior structure}{

The \code{random} formula owns the grouping variables, coefficient or index
design, and covariance family. These prior constructors configure the
already parsed blocks; they do not reinterpret the formula. The \code{id()},
\code{diag()}, and \code{us()} / \code{un()} families use a coefficient formula: \code{1}, \code{0},
and \code{-1} control the random intercept, and continuous slopes, factor slopes,
and interactions are supported. Plain \code{|} syntax defaults to US and \code{||} to
DIAG.

Factor coding in a random-coefficient block is owned by resolved contrast
metadata, not by the presence of an intercept. Existing fixed-factor
contrasts are reused when available; \code{random_block(contrasts = ...)} supplies
an explicit block-specific override. Thus \code{us(0 + group | study)} combined
with \code{contrasts = c(group = "independent")} gives one correlated coefficient
per factor level and no random intercept. Omitting the intercept alone does
not force a level-indicator basis.

The \code{cs()} / \code{hcs()}, \code{ar1()} / \code{ar()} / \code{har()}, and \code{car()} structures
instead own an index basis. Their blocks reject intercept controls and
\code{random_block(contrasts = ...)}. HCS estimates one common pairwise
correlation; US estimates an unrestricted correlation matrix among the
selected coefficient columns. See \code{\link[=random_effect_formula_tags]{random_effect_formula_tags()}} for the
full formula and index-type contract.
}

\subsection{RoBMA completion of partial specifications}{

A named \code{random_block()} must match the block name produced by the parsed
formula. Use \code{only_priors = TRUE} when constructing a \code{\link[=brma.mv]{brma.mv()}} object and
then \code{print_prior(object, parameter = "random")} to inspect the resolved
names and completed specification. An ordinary positive prior supplied to
\code{prior_heterogeneity} changes RoBMA's base heterogeneity prior while retaining
its automatic allocation and covariance rules. A \code{prior_random()} object with
no SD or allocation specification is completed in the same way: RoBMA adds
its UISD-scaled base SD and any required Dirichlet allocation while retaining
the supplied block contrasts, covariance priors, and policies. If any global
or block SD, SD source, term-specific SD override, covariance-owned SD, or
allocation is supplied, the user owns the complete scale architecture and
RoBMA does not merge in scale defaults.

Consequently, covariance-only, contrast-only, monitoring-only,
prediction-only, and parameterization-only objects are partial and
self-finishing in
RoBMA. For example,
\code{prior_random(study = random_block(contrasts = c(group = "independent")))}
needs no explicit SD, allocation, or LKJ prior for a US block. In contrast,
adding \code{sd = ...} makes the object complete; every other required scale or
allocation must then be supplied explicitly.
}

\subsection{Standard-deviation and correlation priors}{

Standard-deviation priors must have nonnegative support. Scalar-correlation
structures use \code{cor} in \code{random_covariance()}; \code{cor_scale = "fisher_z"}
places the prior on Fisher's z, whereas \code{cor_scale = "cor"} places it on the
raw correlation. When omitted, CS/HCS receive a uniform prior over
\verb{(-1 / (K - 1), 1)}, AR1/HAR over \verb{(-1, 1)}, and CAR over \verb{(0, 1)}.
Unstructured \code{us()} blocks default to \code{prior_lkj(eta = 1)}. Values of \code{eta}
above one shrink correlations toward zero; \code{eta = 1} is uniform over
correlation matrices.

The covariance family remains formula-owned. \code{random_covariance(structure = ...)} is mainly useful in direct BayesTools workflows; when used with
\code{brma.mv()}, an explicit structure must match the parsed formula family.
Use \code{prior_lkj()} as \code{cor} for an unstructured matrix prior and an ordinary
scalar prior as \code{cor} for a scalar-correlation structure.
}

\subsection{Variance allocation}{

A block allocation with \code{scale = "total_variance"} exposes \code{sd_total} and
\code{var_total} and splits that total variance across multiple random-effect
blocks:
\deqn{\sigma_j = \sigma_{\mathrm{total}}\sqrt{w_j}, \qquad
      \sum_j \sigma_j^2 = \sigma_{\mathrm{total}}^2.}
An SD-component allocation with
\code{scale = "mean_variance"} instead exposes \code{sd_common} and \code{var_common} and
gives each component the common-SD scale at equal weights:
\deqn{\sigma_j = \sigma_{\mathrm{common}}\sqrt{K w_j}, \qquad
      K^{-1}\sum_j \sigma_j^2 = \sigma_{\mathrm{common}}^2.}
The corresponding public multipliers are \code{var_mult(j) = K * w[j]} and
\code{sd_mult(j) = sqrt(K * w[j])}.
This is useful for heterogeneous level-specific SDs. Root allocations own an
SD prior or external SD source. Child allocations inherit one named parent
component through \code{allocation_ref()} and therefore must not supply another
SD source. \code{inclusion} optionally places independent Bernoulli gates on
named block-allocation components.
}

\subsection{Public parameter names}{

Random-effect quantities are exposed through the semantic catalog view of
BayesTools' fitted parameter map. Concrete posterior coordinates are a linked
view of the same map and are not additional public names. BayesTools
canonical names use \verb{(formula) owner: quantity(arguments)}. RoBMA prints and
accepts simplified aliases: a sole random intercept is \code{sd} for a bare block
and \code{study: sd} for a named block; a non-intercept coefficient remains
explicit, for example \code{study: sd(x)}. An owner-free \code{sd} is accepted only
when it resolves uniquely. Public correlations use \code{cor}; compact scalar
\code{rho} and LKJ construction coordinates remain internal backend dependencies.

A bare formula or unnamed one-entry list omits a redundant component owner.
An explicitly named one-entry list retains its owner. For multiple
components, public component names come from the formula list; missing names
are generated as \verb{component 1}, \verb{component 2}, and so on.
Total-variance allocations expose \code{sd_total}, \code{var_total}, and
\code{var_prop(...)}. Mean-variance allocations expose \code{sd_common}, \code{var_common},
\code{var_mult(...)}, and \code{sd_mult(...)}.
}

\subsection{Monitoring, prediction, and parameterization}{

Keep the default \code{random_monitor(latent = TRUE)} when bridge sampling or
conditional prediction is required. For new grouping levels,
\code{random_new_levels("error")} is the safe default; \code{"zero"} removes their
random contribution and \code{"sample"} draws from the fitted random-effect
distribution.

RoBMA creates external SD sources automatically for its location-scale
random-effect models. Use \code{random_sd_source()} directly only when a custom
complete specification intentionally references a JAGS node created elsewhere
in the model being fitted.

\code{parameterization} changes only the sampled backend representation.
\code{"noncentered"} samples standardized latent effects, \code{"centered"} samples
realized group coefficients, and \code{"auto"} asks BayesTools to choose from the
resolved design. It does not change any SD, allocation, or correlation
prior. Unsupported centered configurations are rejected; \code{"auto"} can fall
back to noncentering.
}
}
\examples{
sd_prior <- prior(
  "normal",
  parameters = list(mean = 0, sd = 0.5),
  truncation = list(lower = 0, upper = Inf)
)

# One random coefficient per group level, with unrestricted correlation.
random_formula <- ~ us(0 + group | study)
unstructured_prior <- prior_random(
  study = random_block(
    contrasts = c(group = "independent")
  )
)

# The same coefficient basis can instead have a diagonal covariance.
diagonal_formula <- ~ diag(0 + group | study)
diagonal_prior <- prior_random(
  study = random_block(
    contrasts = c(group = "independent")
  )
)

# Correlation-only customization remains partial: RoBMA supplies the SDs.
cor_prior <- prior("normal", list(mean = 0, sd = 1))
hcs_prior <- prior_random(
  study = random_block(
    covariance = random_covariance(
      cor = cor_prior,
      cor_scale = "fisher_z"
    )
  )
)

# Allocate one common SD scale across a block's level-specific SD components.
component_allocation <- random_variance_allocation(
  name = "heterogeneity",
  display_name = "",
  terms = "study",
  sd = sd_prior,
  weights = prior("dirichlet", list(alpha = c(1, 1))),
  target = "sd_component",
  scale = "mean_variance"
)
allocated_prior <- prior_random(
  study = random_block(
    covariance = random_covariance(cor = prior_lkj(eta = 1)),
    contrasts = c(group = "independent")
  ),
  allocation = component_allocation
)

# A complete AR prior owns both its SD and its scalar correlation prior.
ar_cor_prior <- prior("normal", list(mean = 0, sd = 0.5))
autoregressive_prior <- prior_random(
  study = random_block(
    sd = sd_prior,
    covariance = random_covariance(
      cor = ar_cor_prior,
      cor_scale = "fisher_z"
    )
  )
)

# Monitoring and new-level prediction policies are explicit when customized.
prediction_prior <- prior_random(
  sd = sd_prior,
  monitor = random_monitor(latent = TRUE, coefficients = FALSE),
  new_levels = random_new_levels(method = "sample")
)

# Advanced: refer to an existing row-shaped model node as the SD source.
external_sd <- random_sd_source("tau", shape = "row")

}
